Conestoga finishes are intended for interior use only. Exterior grade finish materials must be used for exterior cabinetry.

The chart below indicates how much a 12" wide panel of various exterior species can expand as a result of an increase in moisture content
from 5% to 14%. For example, the chart reveals that a Teak panel that is 12" wide could expand by more than 3 16".
A lower value, as shown in the chart below, is directly related to the dimensional stability of the panel. For example, Teak (12.20") and
Thermally Treated Poplar (12.11") are more dimensionally stable and consequently have a reduced risk of bow warp twist than the other
species listed on this chart; however, bow warp twist are never eliminated. As shown in the chart on page 3.4 in the Custom Manual and
below, Hard Maple is used as the baseline for wood expansion; however, it is not intended to be used as an external specie.
Wood expansion caused by an increase in moisture content
12" wide sample of wood at 5% moisture content ... and at 14%
Thermally-Treated Poplar -  12.11"
Teak -  12.20"
Cypress - 12.23"
Mahogany (Sapele) -  12.26"
Hard Maple - 12.38"
